### What an earlier round found at these commits

An earlier round's evaluator reported these anomalies at exactly the commits you evaluate now, each with its round,
where it was seen and its words, quoted:

{{fence . | chomp}}

An anomaly is a fact about the commits it was found at, and nothing has changed since: each one keeps maul from
confirming this change, whatever you find, until the commits change or an approver accepts it. Examine each one again
before you settle on any verdict: read the code it names and try to reproduce it with maul-tool, using data of your own
the saved checks do not use. Where you reproduce it, report it again under `anomalies`, citing this round's ledger, and
where an approved outcome covers the behaviour it names, judge that outcome on what you ran, not on its saved checks
alone. Where you cannot reproduce it, say in your report's text what you ran and what you saw: the approver reads it
beside the anomaly.
